WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:10.090
To understand how acids react with bases,
we take about 2 millilitres of sodium

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:14.850
hydroxide solution in a testtube and add a
couple of drops of phenolphathalein

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.880
solution to it.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.900
What do you notice about the color?

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.269
Yes, the colour of the solution turns pink!

00:00:22.269 --> 00:00:28.400
The indicator phenolphthalein is colourless
in acidic solutions, but goes pink in

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.909
basic solutions!

00:00:29.909 --> 00:00:34.280
As sodium hydroxide is a base, the indicator
turns pink!

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:41.140
Now to see how bases react with acids, we
add hydrochloric acid to this drop by

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.170
drop!

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:43.170
What do you observe?

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:46.030
We see that the solution turns colourless
again.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.170
So the
colourless base turned pink and then again

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.620
turned colourless after an acid was
added.

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.190
What does this prove?

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:58.609
We can say that the effect of a base is neutralised
or nullified by an acid.

00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:01.280
Does the reverse also happen?

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.070
Will an acid be neutralised by a base?

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:08.210
To find out,
let’s add a few drops of sodium hydroxide

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.680
back to the above mixture.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.340
And yes, the
pink colour of the phenolphthalein reappears!

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.509
That’s because the effect of acid is
neutralised by the base!

00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:22.760
So the reaction taking place here can be written
like this:

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:26.500
Sodium chloride is nothing but a salt.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.659
So an acid and a base react to give us salt
and water!

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:31.980
And guess what such a reaction is called?

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.880
It’s called a Neutralisation
reaction!

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:38.690
A base reacts with acid to give us salt and
water.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:42.640
Base + Acid  Salt + Water
In a similar way, calcium chloride is formed

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.520
along with water when hydrochloric
the acid reacts with calcium hydroxide!

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:50.329
2HCl + Ca(OH) 2 &nbsp; CaCl 2 &nbsp;+ 2H 2 O
Can you think of a more practical reaction?

00:01:50.329 --> 00:01:55.950
When we have acidity, our stomach
produces excess acid which is nothing but

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.100
hydrochloric acid(HCl).

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.159
And this acid is
very harmful for the walls of our stomach…

00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:03.850
which is why we get that burning
sensation.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:08.590
So to neutralize the acid, what we use is
an antacid.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.760
Antacids as you
would have guessed are nothing but bases!

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.950
The antacid reacts with the base to
give salt and water.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:16.580
And that gives us relief!

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.540
Base + HCl  Salt + Water

